{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"Understanding body mechanics and physics in SFM means timing frames to force, mass, and follow-through rather than evenly spaced keys.\n  Weight shift starts the motion when the center of mass moves.\n    Ground contact sets push-off, stop, and impact frames.\n      Mass and inertia determine how many frames acceleration and deceleration need.\n        Gravity shapes vertical arcs, making falls faster than rises.\n          Limb hierarchy makes the torso lead while arms and legs follow through.\n            Joint limits and anatomy set safe earliest and latest transition frames.\n              Torque creates curved rotational timing around a pivot.\n                Impact, squash, recoil, cloth, and hair timing must match collision force.\n                  Anticipation frames show the body preparing before the main action.\n                    In SFM, key at push, contact, peak, and recovery, then adjust frame counts to fit the physical event.","reasoning_content":"User asks: \"in SFM, how exactly can understanding body mechanics and physics influence timing in frames? Create stairs by incrementally indenting each new line.\"\n\nNeed respond with content about SFM (Source Filmmaker) and body mechanics/physics influencing timing in frames. Also constraint: \"Create stairs by incrementally indenting each new line.\" Means each new line should be indented more than previous line, likely a staircase of increasing leading spaces. Need ensure each line starts with indentation increasing. Could be each line has more spaces/tabs than previous. Need answer in English? User in English. Need final only, with stairs. Need likely use lines each more indented. Need maybe maintain answer coherent. Let's design maybe 12-20 lines. Each line incrementally indented by, say, 2 spaces more than previous. Need no blank lines? Blank lines could break? If blank line no content maybe indentation not visible. Better avoid b