vement should start/stop.\n- acceleration/deceleration from mass affects how many frames a transition takes.\n- follow-through, overlap, and secondary motion determine later frames.\n- anticipation and recovery from force change pose timing.\n- collision/impact timing must align with contact points and momentum.\n- gravity and free fall dictate vertical positions per frame.\n- joint limits and limb hierarchy cause different limbs to lead/lag.\n- torque/rotational inertia changes speed around axes, affecting arc timing.\n- squash/stretch and deformation timing reflects impact energy.\n- frame-by-frame keying can place keys at mechanical cause (muscle push, ground contact) rather than arbitrary equal intervals.\n- understanding physics lets you choose shorter frame counts for light/fast limbs, longer for heavy/massive moves, and match easing to force.\n\nNeed format: stairs by incrementally indenting each new line. Could do each line a phrase. Need ensure each new line indented more. Let's create 14 lines, each more indented than previous. Use 2 spaces increments? But after 4 spaces code block? If use 2 spaces increments, line 3 4 spaces -> code block from line 3? Actually markdown code block requires 4 spaces from start; line 3 would be code block if not preceded by text? If first line is paragraph, second line 2 spaces continues paragraph, third line 4 spaces may create code block? It could be messy. But user likely just wants staircase. Maybe use 1 space increments to 15 lines: 0,1,2,...14 spaces. Line 5 has 4 spaces. Could be code block. But if all lines are code block? The first line not code. Hmm.\n\nCould use 1 half-width space increments? If many lines, 14 spaces. Code block may start at 4. Could make first line a non-answer? If code fence, but then every line in fence has base indentation plus incremental. For example:\n```text\n\n  a\n    b\n      c\n```\nThe code fence lines not part of content. But user said create stairs by indenting each new line. The co